<!--
  AP session-tx buffer-pool config, SMALL class: the slots a tokio (AP / Linux)
  DATAGRAM link's outbound frames live in when they are no larger than the
  datagram the sessions actually send (ARCHITECTURE section 9.1). The LARGE
  class beside it is `session_tx_pool_ap.scxml`, the stream links' pool, whose
  slot holds the largest frame any AP link can be handed; a datagram link owns
  one instance of each, and a frame takes the smallest class it fits.

  Born with its consumers: `wz-runtime-tokio`'s datagram writer queue
  (`writer_queue::outbound_channel_datagram`), behind `runtime-zero-copy`, for
  the UDP, QUIC-datagram, websocket and multicast links. A datagram is its own
  boundary, so a slot carries ONE frame and frames are never packed.

  ## slot-size 1472 is DERIVED from what a datagram link sends

  The session sizes a datagram to `min(link mtu, negotiated batch)`
  (`SessionLinkActions::negotiated_batch_mtu`), and the link mtus are:
    - UDP unicast: `UDP_LINK_MTU` = 1450, zenoh-pico's own figure
      (`_z_get_link_mtu_udp_unicast`), so that a datagram fits one Ethernet
      frame without IP fragmentation;
    - QUIC datagram: the connection's `max_datagram_size`, floored at
      `QUIC_DATAGRAM_LINK_MTU` = 1200; quinn's path-MTU discovery stops at an
      upper bound of 1452 bytes of UDP payload, of which the QUIC packet header
      and the DATAGRAM frame header take their share, so the datagram is
      below 1452;
    - multicast and websocket: the negotiated batch, up to 65535, which is the
      LARGE class's case when a frame is that big.
  So the frames a datagram link sends in steady state are at most 1450 bytes,
  and 1450 rounded up to the 64-byte alignment below is 1472.

  ## slot-count 728 is DERIVED from the queue's own byte bound

  The writer queue bounds each lane by `queue_size * batch_bytes` bytes, and a
  datagram link's per-link slot budget for a class is, per lane,
  `ceil(queue_size * batch_bytes / slot_size) + 1` (the lane's bound filled
  with frames of the slot's size, plus the one frame that may take the lane
  past its bound), summed over the lanes. That budget is a CONFIGURATION input:
  it follows `transport/link/tx/queue/size/*` and the link's batch size through
  the shape the established session gives its queue. This count is the largest
  budget the defaults can ask for, the queue before its session is
  established: 8 lanes, queue size 2, batch 65535, so
  8 * (ceil(131070 / 1472) + 1) = 8 * (90 + 1) = 728.
  An established UDP session without QoS asks for 1 * (ceil(2 * 1450 / 1472)
  + 1) = 3, and with QoS 8 * 3 = 24: the bound in bytes zenoh's two batches
  per priority hold, counted in datagrams. A shape whose budget is past this
  count is clamped to it (raising the count is a change here, not a knob).

  At 728 x 1472 the class is about 1 MiB of address space per datagram link,
  allocated zeroed once and resident only where frames were written.

  ## section / alignment / cache-policy

  `heap` / 64 / `none`, as the other AP pools.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="session_tx_pool_ap_small" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>728</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>1472</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>heap</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>64</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>none</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
